nodecode-secret-guard is a rewrite of ChasClogston's ChasClogston/hermes-secret-guard:

  https://github.com/ChasClogston/hermes-secret-guard
  commit b823a9ebeca0847a6c7a5bd73f0678d6b4657924
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Charles Clogston (LICENSE)

Rewritten: the store's three layers, in Common Lisp against the Nodecode cell
contract. A key is held under the name a tool reads it by and answers a
fingerprint and a length, never its value (store.lisp). The gate on a call
that reads a credential store -- a secret-shaped file, a keychain or
password-manager print, the whole environment dumped -- became an advice on
Nodecode's :TOOL point, reading every string a call carries instead of the
argument names the original keyed on (guard.lisp). The scrub of every tool
result, longest value first, with the original's `[secret:NAME redacted]`
marker and its own ordering, is the same advice's other half. The model-facing
tool that asks for a key without taking one, the registry listing and the
CLI's add/list/verify/remove became three tools and /secret.

Left out, and why: the macOS keychain itself, because Nodecode runs on Linux,
macOS and Windows and no one keychain API spans them -- the value lives in one
file under the Nodecode home, mode 600, replaced atomically, and the registry
the original kept beside its keychain is the same file's other members. The
hidden-input dialog became an operator command (/secret add NAME SOURCE), since
a Nodecode tool answers text and only a command can carry a panel; the value
still never passes through the model. The per-secret scope (all / default-only
for messaging-bot tokens) is dropped, because a store belongs to one home and
every lane of that home is the same person. The check on the operator's own
message for a pasted key has no seam here, so the model is given secret_check
to read its drafts with instead. The `import` helper-script reader and the
read-only `doctor` are left out: the store is one JSON file a person can read.
